From 5854cbd369f28632c337a1d5b35044ac7a619c31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: choiceoh <choiceoh@topsolar.kr>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 09:03:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] perf(engine): split a threshold bin wider than the stash once
 more before refining against the row

GB10 blocks hold 48 KB of shared memory, so past 24,576 candidates the decode
selection keeps no bin cache and a 2,560-slot stash. A threshold bin wider than
that refined the 64-bit key a byte at a time against the row -- up to eight more
reads of the row, most of them spent on bytes every element of the bin shares:
228 us against a 12 us read floor at 8x131,072 (measurements/st_topk_hpcops_20260917).

The bin now fixes an fp32 key range (the fp16 rounding midpoints around its code
range, an ulp wider each side; exponents 0, 1, 30, 31 take the whole key space).
One pass histograms the bin's elements over that range cut into 256; the sift
sends winners above the cut sub-bin out and only the cut sub-bin to the stash.
The old row refinement stays for a sub-bin still wider than the stash (a plateau).
The sub-bin index is clamped to the range, which is monotone, so the set and the
lower-pool-id tie rule are unchanged; hist is reused, no shared memory is added.

CPU: the range, shift and clamp are mirrored and checked on both sides of every
fp16 rounding midpoint (a planted range error is caught). The comparison probe
gains narrow, negative, sparse and lognormal distributions and times narrow too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
